---
import CaseStudy from '../../../components/CaseStudy.astro';

const study = {
    metaDescription:
        'How a Victorian law practice moved from controls left in audit mode to one enforced control set covering VLSB+C expectations, the Essential Eight, SMB1001 and NIST CSF.',
    sector: 'Law practice',
    title: 'Switched on, but not enforced',
    summary:
        'A Victorian law practice had multi-factor authentication, application control and Conditional Access in place. On paper, it was well protected. In practice, several of those controls were left in audit mode, recording what they would have blocked without blocking anything. About four months later, one enforced set of controls covered four frameworks, the firm could give clients and insurers evidence within 24 hours, and it opened a new office on the same secure foundation.',
    glance: [
        ['Client', 'Victorian law practice'],
        ['Scale', 'Under 30 staff on Microsoft 365'],
        ['Starting point', "Endpoint protection and basic security, broadly meeting the Victorian Legal Services Board and Commissioner's (VLSB+C) minimum expectations. Several key controls in audit mode. No security strategy."],
        ['Why', 'To go beyond the regulatory minimum ahead of rising client expectations, and to open new offices quickly without weakening security.'],
        ['Timeline', 'About four months from first call to full implementation'],
        ['Result', [
            'Controls previously in audit mode now enforced',
            'One control set aligned with VLSB+C expectations, the Essential Eight, SMB1001 and NIST CSF',
            'Each control set at the level that suits the firm, not to chase a single rating',
            'A security strategy with defined goals',
            'Security evidence available to third parties within 24 hours',
            'A satellite office opened on the same controls',
        ]],
    ],
    sections: {
        background: 'Why the firm acted',
        findings: 'What we found',
        method: 'How we approached it',
        outcomes: 'What changed',
    },
    background: [
        "Every Victorian law practice is expected to meet the VLSB+C's minimum cyber security expectations. This firm didn't want to stop there. Client expectations were rising, and it wanted to be ready for new risks rather than react to them.",
        "It was also planning to grow, but had no clear view of its security posture and was concerned that security wouldn't hold up through that change. It asked Decoheron for an approach aligned with three frameworks: the ASD Essential Eight, SMB1001 (a tiered cyber security standard for small business) and the NIST Cybersecurity Framework (CSF).",
    ],
    findings: [
        { lead: 'Controls were configured, not enforced.', text: 'Multi-factor authentication, application control and Conditional Access were in place, but several were in audit or report-only mode. They logged threats instead of stopping them.' },
        { lead: "Meeting the minimum wasn't proving anything.", text: "Basic security broadly met the VLSB+C expectations, but didn't demonstrate alignment with any recognised framework." },
        { lead: 'There was no strategy.', text: "Without a clear view of its posture, the firm's growth plans were held back by uncertainty about whether security would survive the change." },
    ],
    method: {
        columns: ['Framework', 'Target', 'Role in the program'],
        rows: [
            ['VLSB+C expectations', 'Meet in full', 'The regulatory floor: the system and behavioural controls expected of every Victorian law practice'],
            ['ASD Essential Eight', 'Level chosen per strategy', 'The technical baseline: application control, patching, admin privileges, multi-factor authentication and backups'],
            ['SMB1001', 'Level chosen per control', 'A recognised, tiered benchmark the firm can show clients and insurers'],
            ['NIST CSF', 'Program structure', 'Governance and risk structure across six functions: Govern, Identify, Protect, Detect, Respond and Recover'],
            ['Control set', 'One, mapped to all four', 'Governance, identity, privilege, applications, procedures and hardening'],
        ],
    },
    stats: [
        { from: 'Audit mode', figure: 'Enforced', label: 'MFA, application control and Conditional Access', note: 'Protection no longer depends on someone reading a report.' },
        { from: '4', figure: '1', label: 'Frameworks covered by one control set', note: 'VLSB+C expectations, Essential Eight, SMB1001 and NIST CSF.' },
        { figure: '24 hours', label: 'To produce security evidence', note: 'For clients, insurers and other third parties, via VeriState.' },
    ],
    outcomes: [
        {
            title: 'For the partners',
            text: "The firm runs one security program instead of four. One control set covers governance, identity, privilege, applications, procedures and hardening across every framework it cares about. As it expands into jurisdictions where SMB1001 Gold is already recommended, it's positioned to respond quickly.",
        },
        {
            title: 'For clients',
            text: 'When a client or insurer asks how the firm protects their information, it can provide evidence of its current security posture within 24 hours through Veristate. Because its controls are already mapped to the Essential Eight, SMB1001 and NIST CSF, new client requirements are a mapping exercise, not a new project.',
        },
        {
            title: 'For growth',
            text: "Devices deploy zero-touch, meaning they're ready to use on first sign-in with no manual setup. Personal mobiles are supported securely, with access limited to email and Teams. That's what let the firm open its satellite office quickly, on the same enforced controls as head office. It also now has a security strategy with defined goals for continual improvement.",
        },
    ],
    closing: {
        title: 'Are your controls enforced, or just enabled?',
        text: 'Audit mode looks the same as protection until something gets through. A fixed-fee assessment shows which of your controls are actually doing their job.',
    },
};
---
<!-- PREPARER NOTE: consider updating the URL slug to /case-studies/law-practice-enforced-controls to match the new title, with a redirect from the old URL. -->
<CaseStudy study={study} />
